sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/clade-trajectory-universality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Are clade diversity trajectories universally hat-shaped?" ;
    dct:description "Do most fossil clades rise to a diversity peak and decline symmetrically, as Darwin and later Foote and Quental and Marshall proposed, or is the apparent hat shape a product of sampling and the way clades are defined?" ;

    skos:historyNote "Known state: Darwin claimed groups rise gradually to a maximum then decline (ch10-06; repo verdict mixed). Foote (2007) found symmetric waxing and waning of marine invertebrate genera; Liow and Stenseth (2007) found similar shapes in mammals; Quental and Marshall (2013) linked decline to Red Queen dynamics. Critics attribute symmetry to the 'pull of the recent', paraphyletic clade definitions and sampling heterogeneity. No standardized, subsampling-corrected test across all orders and families exists." ;
    dct:subject "biology" ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "open-problem/tier-B" ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolved when: A pre-registered test over all PBDB orders and families meeting stated occurrence thresholds, using shareholder-quorum subsampling and monophyly filters, with a stated statistic for hat-shapedness and a null from randomized origination-extinction sequences." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolved when: A verdict with effect size: fraction of clades significantly hat-shaped versus the null." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Refuted if: If the fraction of hat-shaped clades is indistinguishable from the random-walk null after subsampling, the pattern is an artifact." ;
    skos:note "Data access: Paleobiology Database API (open), already wired into test-data/organisms." ;
    skos:note "Requires: pbdb" ;
    skos:note "Requires: paleobiology-statistics" ;
    skos:note "Requires: subsampling" ;
    skos:note "Requires: data-test" ;
    skos:note "Scale: evolutionary" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch10-02> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1666/06084.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2007.1006> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1239431> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/e77f4bcffb077981b60f1e3aea24b72c7a36b2fc/problems/problems.json> .

  <https://doi.org/10.1666/06084.1> dct:title "Symmetric waxing and waning of marine invertebrate genera" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Foote 2007, Symmetric waxing and waning of marine invertebrate genera" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2007.1006> dct:title "The rise and fall of species: implications for macroevolutionary and macroecological studies" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Liow and Stenseth 2007, The rise and fall of species: implications for macroevolutionary and macroecological studies" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1239431> dct:title "How the Red Queen Drives Terrestrial Mammals to Extinction" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Quental and Marshall 2013, How the Red Queen drives terrestrial mammals to extinction" .
}

